# Contributing to Skein

This file designates the official repository and states the
submission mechanics referenced in ILL (Sections 4 and 5 of
the LICENSE file, ILL-1.0).

================================================================
1. OFFICIAL REPOSITORY
================================================================

The official Product repository is:

    https://github.com/aiv-tmc/skein

Pull requests to this repository are Submitted Modifications.
If several repositories are designated as official for the
current Product release, a pull request to any of them
suffices; the list of official repositories is kept up to
date in this file.

Public publication of a Modification in your own repository,
without a pull request to the official repository or an
explicit consent mark, does not trigger the grants described
in section 2.

================================================================
2. WHAT YOU GRANT BY SUBMITTING
================================================================

By giving a consent mark (section 3) for a Modification, you
grant the Authors the following, with respect to that
Submitted Modification:

(a) a gratuitous (royalty-free), irrevocable, worldwide,
    non-exclusive license for the entire term of copyright,
    with the right to sublicense, to use, reproduce, modify,
    distribute, make publicly available and commercially
    license the Submitted Modification as part of the ILL
    Files, the Product and any Derivative Works, in any
    manner and for any purpose, including commercial
    purposes;
(b) a gratuitous, irrevocable, worldwide, non-exclusive
    patent license under your patents, to the extent
    necessary to use the Submitted Modification as part of
    the Product and its Derivative Works;
(c) the assurance that moral rights will not be asserted to
    the maximum extent permitted by applicable law;
(d) you retain copyright in original code you created and
    may use it independently, provided such code
    functionally works without the ILL Files.

Note on the grant in section 2: it is intentionally broader
than a typical DCO sign-off - it is irrevocable, includes
the right to sublicense and to commercially license your
contribution, and it survives your withdrawal from the
project. Do not give the consent mark if you do not accept
this.

================================================================
3. HOW TO SUBMIT (CONSENT MARK)
================================================================

Small contributions (500 lines of code or fewer):
    - sign off the commit with the Developer Certificate of
      Origin 1.1 (developercertificate.org), e.g.:
          Signed-off-by: Your Name <e-mail>
      The sign-off certifies that you have the right to
      submit the contribution and constitutes the consent
      mark, in lieu of the consent checkbox.

Large contributions (more than 500 lines of code):
    - additionally sign the contributor license agreement in
      the form published by the Authors (if any), and attach
      it to the pull request.

Repository interface:
    - on your first submission a separate consent checkbox
      is shown; checking it is an explicit consent mark.
      Using the checkbox or a DCO sign-off - one of the two -
      is mandatory for every contribution.

================================================================
4. AUTHORSHIP CHAIN
================================================================

Every accepted contribution is added to the Authorship Chain
(AUTHORS/NOTICE file and, where customary, per-file
headers): your name or stable pseudonym, the date or
version, and a brief description of the changes. The chain
is never shortened or rewritten; see Section 4 of the
License.

================================================================
5. PRACTICAL RULES
================================================================

- Base your work on the current release branch; keep pull
  requests focused and reviewable.
- Do not remove copyright notices, license references or
  chain entries (ILL Sections 4 and 11).
- Modifications must inherit the License Stack: the ILL
  base license plus all Modules of previous Authors (ILL
  Sections 5 and 6).
- You may attach a Module (Supplemental Terms) only to your
  own original additions and only in accordance with ILL
  Section 6; it must not contradict the base License or any
  previous Module, must not grant commercial rights, and
  must not impose fees for Non-Commercial use.
- Keep Third-Party Components under their own licenses; do
  not link or combine them with the ILL Files in a way that
  would subject the ILL Files to copyleft (ILL Section 2).
- Contributions containing third-party code must identify it
  and state its license in the pull request description.
- Automated checks (security, style, license headers, chain
  completeness) run on every pull request.

Questions: the contact stated in the AUTHORS file.

================================================================
6. UNCONFIRMED CONTRIBUTIONS (ILL Section 1.8)
================================================================

A contribution merged without a consent mark is not an
ILL File and is not licensed under ILL until its chain
entry is confirmed. Maintainers must mark such entries
"unconfirmed" and must not include such code in a
release Distribution until confirmed.
